From d386ccc01ed74b780b387b786ea862d41c61588f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: A9 project <dmayala@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 10:07:15 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] drm/a9_eink: frame-progress interlock and bin admission;
 regulator: fp9929a: emergency off

Review 7 (finding 3) showed that a nonzero drive frame left on the glass
is only bounded by the FP9929A whole-session max_on_ms timer: if the
worker stops, a frame is late, or drm_client_modeset_commit() blocks
while a drive buffer scans out, one polarity can be held for seconds.
Finding 2 showed that the complete Clear request is 60-208 frames across
the 14 temperature bins, so the frame envelope has to be admitted per
bin before any drive.

fp9929a: fp9929a_emergency_off(why), exported, callable from any context
including hardirq/hrtimer (no lock, no sleep). It latches an emergency
fault, closes allow_power_on, drives VCOM-EN low at once (gpiod_set_value;
TLMM does not sleep, as for the panic notifier) and queues a work on
system_highpri_wq 50 ms later that, under the gate lock but without
fp->lock (the stuck party may hold it), drops VCOM-EN again (a power-up
that had passed its gate check may have raised it) and PWRUP, and marks
the rails off. If VCOM-EN was raised after the first drop, the stock 50 ms
passes again before PWRUP. queue_delayed_work, not mod: repeated calls do
not postpone the drop. While latched, power-up fails with -EIO at entry
and at every gate check (before PWRUP, before VCOM-EN, in and after the
PG wait). Only writing 1 to fp9929a.clear_fault clears it, and that write
fails with -EBUSY while the delayed drop is still pending. A normal
disable does not clear it. fp9929a_get_status() reports it (emergency).

a9_eink transport: an hrtimer (HRTIMER_MODE_REL_HARD) per transport,
(re)armed with 3 frame periods (A9X_STALL_FRAMES x period_us, ~35 ms)
BEFORE every commit of a non-null frame and cancelled
(hrtimer_try_to_cancel) after every SUCCESSFUL commit of a null one
(a9x_show of a null frame, a9x_blank, the power-up and power-down zero
commits). It is therefore armed exactly while a drive frame may be on the
glass and no newer commit has started. 3 periods: on boot 11 zero-frame
commits ran with a maximum gap of 13.2 ms, the dry run computed a frame
in ~3.3 ms; a gap above 3 periods means >= 2 extra refreshes (drive
pulses) of one frame beyond the 1.5-period "late" threshold. On expiry
(hardirq, no locks): if the transport is on, fp9929a_emergency_off(),
latch a9x_stall_fault, run the driver's hook (enable := 0, so a worker
that resumes aborts), dev_crit.

The power-down now commits a zero frame whenever the interlock is armed
(a drive commit was started, even if it failed: it may have latched), not
only when front_null is false. If that zero commit fails, it is a fault:
emergency off + latch, then DPMS off as before. The second initial zero
commit of the power-up is now checked; on failure power-up fails and the
caller powers off. Teardown cancels the timer (a still-armed one is an
emergency).

While a9x_stall_fault is latched, a9_update() refuses (aborts++) and
a9_power_on() returns -EIO. a9_eink.clear_fault (write 1) clears it. The
status sysfs first line gains " stall_fault %d" at its end.

Bin admission: a9_eink.bin_min/bin_max (0..13, default 0/13, min <= max
enforced on write). A real waveform update (not zero_test, not a dry run)
is refused before power-up if the bin of the previous FP9929A TMST sample
(temp_fallback if none) and the fresh PMI632 thermistor is outside the
window, and again after power-up, with this power-up's TMST, before the
first frame (power off, aborts++, "bin N outside [min,max]: update
refused"). Dry runs report it but are not refused. The below-0 C refusal
is kept. Note: TMST is only resampled at a power-up, so a stale
out-of-window sample keeps refusing until the window changes or a
zero_test session (not bin-checked) resamples it.

The fault latch, clear_fault and the bin window live in the new
a9_eink_guard.c so the host harness compiles them unchanged. Host tests:
tests/mainline-fp9929a (cases q, r, r2, r3) and the new
tests/mainline-a9eink-stall (real a9_eink_transport.c + guard against a
mocked DRM commit and pthread hrtimer) in the A9 work repo.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
